    on a 64-bit arch where floats have 53-bit mantissa,
    ivmin and ivmax are promoted floats to rounded to a
    multiple of 2^(64 - 53) = 2048.  so naturally, the
    -1 is lost in the process.

    the careful IV/UV overflow guard i had observed by testing
    integer math turns out to be implemented in commit 16b7a9a47b
    (tags/perl-5.7.1~968, "faster and 64 bit preserving arithmetic")
    that makes it so that, when compiled without -DNO_PERL_PRESERVE
    _IVUV (perl.h:1088), perl would try adding IV/UV together if it
    doesn't overflow (pp_hot.c:384); and ONLY in that case would it
    fall through to NV.

    Previously there was no attempt at remaining integer.

